WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.875
CSP reporting gives teams visibility into
policy violations.

2
00:00:05.955 --> 00:00:12.749
Reports can show that the browser blocked
or would have blocked a resource load,

3
00:00:12.829 --> 00:00:18.509
inline execution attempt, frame,
connection, or other behavior.

4
00:00:18.589 --> 00:00:24.369
report-uri and report-to are reporting
mechanisms at a high level,

5
00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:31.345
with browser support and operational
details that teams should verify for their

6
00:00:31.425 --> 00:00:32.642
environment.

7
00:00:32.722 --> 00:00:40.024
Report-only policies are especially useful
during rollout because they reveal what

8
00:00:40.104 --> 00:00:45.377
enforcement would affect before users
experience breakage.

9
00:00:45.457 --> 00:00:48.297
Reports are not perfect signals.

10
00:00:48.377 --> 00:00:54.563
Browser extensions, privacy tools, old
cached pages, unusual clients,

11
00:00:54.643 --> 00:01:02.147
injected enterprise tooling, and harmless
user environment differences can generate

12
00:01:02.227 --> 00:01:02.836
noise.

13
00:01:02.916 --> 00:01:09.913
Some reports may also include URLs or
context that should be treated as sensitive.

14
00:01:09.993 --> 00:01:17.700
Privacy considerations matter because
reporting endpoints receive browser-side event

15
00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:18.287
data.

16
00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:25.162
Teams should collect the minimum useful
data, protect the reporting pipeline,

17
00:01:25.242 --> 00:01:30.211
and avoid treating every report as an
incident by default.

18
00:01:30.291 --> 00:01:33.638
The value of reporting is improvement.

19
00:01:33.718 --> 00:01:37.673
Reports can identify missing policy
entries,

20
00:01:37.753 --> 00:01:43.229
unexpected third-party dependencies, risky
inline behavior,

21
00:01:43.309 --> 00:01:48.481
or pages that still rely on patterns the
team wants to remove.

22
00:01:48.561 --> 00:01:55.153
Monitoring for unexpected resource loads
can also help spot frontend drift.

23
00:01:55.233 --> 00:02:02.332
A useful reporting program has triage
rules, ownership, dashboards or summaries,

24
00:02:02.412 --> 00:02:06.265
and a feedback loop into policy
maintenance.

25
00:02:06.345 --> 00:02:13.140
CSP reports are evidence for tuning the
policy, not a pile of warnings to ignore.
